{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":3,"query":"name=Moss&limit=10&title=Hooked","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Nauvoo to the Hook","subTitle":"the iconography of a barrier beach"},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Moss, George H.","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"},"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nju"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New Jersey"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Locust, N.J."}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Jervey Close Press"},"dateIssued":"1964"},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"128 p. illus., facsims., maps. 32 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"map"}}],"note":{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"by George H. Moss, Jr."},"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us-nj"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Sandy Hook (N.J.)"},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"Monmouth County (N.J.)","topic":"History"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"F142.S25 M6"},{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"974.946"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Nauvoo to the Hook"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"Locust, N.J., Jervey Close Press, 1964"},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)761127680"},"name":{"namePart":"Moss, George H."}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990061174460203941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^64022451^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"4991307"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://id.loc.gov/rwo/agents/no2014094889","#text":"Widener Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"US 16393.6"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Widener"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/990061174460203941","priorrecordids":{"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALEPH","#text":"006117446"}},"processingDate":"2026-08-08T04:17Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"790522"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20020606"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"990061174460203941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)"}},{"titleInfo":[{"title":"Hooked","subTitle":"food, free will, and how the food giants exploit our addictions"},{"@type":"alternative","title":"Food, free will, and how the food giants exploit our addictions"}],"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Moss, Michael",{"@type":"date","#text":"1955-"}],"role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author."}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2022"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2021"},"edition":"Random House Trade Paperback edition.","issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York :"}},"publisher":"Random House,","dateIssued":"2022."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"xxviii, 274 pages ; 21 cm"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"Everyone knows how hard it can be to maintain a healthy diet. But what if some of the decisions we make about what to eat are beyond our control? Is it possible that processed food is addictive, like drugs or alcohol? Motivated by these questions, Pulitzer Prize-winning investigative reporter Michael Moss began searching for answers, to find the true peril in our food. In Hooked, Moss explores the science of addiction and uncovers what the scientific and medical communities--as well as food manufacturers--already know, which is that food can, in some cases, be even more addictive than alcohol, cigarettes, or drugs. Our bodies are hard-wired for sweets, so food manufacturers have deployed fifty-six types of sugar to add to their products, creating in us the expectation that everything should be cloying; we've evolved to prefer convenient meals, so three-fourths of the calories we get from groceries come from ready-to-eat foods. Moss goes on to show how the processed food industry has not only tried to deny this troubling discovery, but exploit it to its advantage. For instance, in a response to recent dieting trends, food manufacturers have simply turned junk food into junk diets, filling grocery stores with \"diet\" foods that are hardly distinguishable from the products that got us into trouble in the first place. With more people unable to make dieting work for them, manufacturers are now claiming to add ingredients that can effortlessly cure our compulsive eating habits. A gripping account of the legal battles, insidious marketing campaigns, and cutting-edge food science that have brought us to our current public health crisis, Hooked lays out all that the food industry is doing to exploit and deepen our addictions, and shows us what we can do so that we can one again seize control\"--"},"tableOfContents":"Prologue: \"I had a food affair\" -- Part one: Inside addiction. \"What's your definition?\" ; \"Where does it begin?\" ; \"It's all related to memory\" ; \"We by nature are drawn to eating\" -- Part two: Outside addiction. \"The variety seekers\" ; \"She is dangerous\" ; \"Give your willpower a boost\" ; \"The blueprint for your DNA\" -- Epilogue: Changing what we value.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Michael Moss."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Nutrition"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Compulsive eating"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food additives","Health aspects"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Convenience foods","Health aspects"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food industry and trade","Marketing"],"geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Nutritional Physiological Phenomena"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Binge-Eating Disorder"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Nutrition"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":["Alimentation","Comportement compulsif"]},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"BUSINESS & ECONOMICS / Corporate & Business History"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"HEALTH & FITNESS / Diet & Nutrition / Nutrition"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"PSYCHOLOGY / Psychopathology / Addiction"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Compulsive eating"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":["Food additives","Health aspects"]},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Food industry and trade"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":["Food industry and trade","Marketing"]},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Nutrition"},{"@authority":"fast","geographic":"United States"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"RA784 .M67 2021"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"WM 175"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"613.2"},{"@authority":"siso","#text":"678"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Hooked"},"name":{"namePart":"Moss, Michael, 1955-"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York : Random House, [2021]","edition":"First edition."},"identifier":[{"@type":"local","#text":"(DLC)  2020012081"},{"@type":"local","#text":"(OCoLC)1145088836"}]},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99156132029403941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0812987136"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780812987133"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1274200772"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","#text":"Science and Engineering Complex Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"RA784 .M67 2021"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Science and Engineering Complex Library"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/99156132029403941","processingDate":"2024-04-18T05:15Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"YDX"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"211012"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20240208"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99156132029403941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Salt, sugar, fat ","subTitle":"how the food giants hooked us "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Moss, Michael,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1955-"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Popular Work"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"dateIssued":[{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2013"},"c2013"],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Random House"},"edition":"1st ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xxx, 446 p. ; 25 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"The author explores his theory that the food industry's used three essential ingredients to control much of the world's diet. Traces the rise of the processed food industry and how addictive salt, sugar, and fat have enabled its dominance in the past half century, revealing deliberate corporate practices behind current trends in obesity, diabetes, and other health challenges. Features examples from some of the most recognizable and profitable companies and brands of the last half century, including Kraft, Coca-Cola, Lunchables, Kellogg, Frito-Lay, Nestlé, Oreos, Cargill, Capri Sun, and many more."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"\"The company jewels\" -- Sugar -- \"Exploiting the biology of the child\" -- \"How do you get people to crave?\" -- \"Convenience with a Capital 'C'\" -- \"Is it cereal or candy?\" -- \"I want to see a lot of body bags\" -- \"A burst of fruity aroma\" -- Fat -- \"That gooey, sticky mouthfeel\" -- \"Liquid gold\" -- \"Lunchtime is all yours\" -- \"The message the government conveys\" -- \"No sugar, no fat, no sales\" -- Salt -- \"People love salt\" -- \"The same great salty taste your customers crave\" -- \"I feel so sorry for the public\" -- \"We're hooked on inexpensive food.\""},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Michael Moss."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (p. [357]-422) and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Sodium Chloride, Dietary","adverse effects"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Feeding Behavior"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Dietary Sucrose","adverse effects"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Dietary Fats","adverse effects"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Food Industry","economics"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Diet","adverse effects"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Nutritional Physiological Phenomena"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Food Industry"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Feeding Behavior"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Diet","economics"]},{"@authority":"mesh","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Nutrition","Economic aspects"],"geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food habits","Economic aspects"],"geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Food industry and trade","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food industry and trade","Corrupt practices"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food industry and trade","Moral and ethical aspects"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Corporations","Corrupt practices"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food habits","Health aspects"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Food additives"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Food additives","Toxicology"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Junk food"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Compulsive behavior"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Compulsive eating"},{"@authority":"mesh","geographic":"United States"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"RA784 .M638 2013"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"2013 C-611"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"QT 2